[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Request and Response Lifecycle in Express.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every interaction with an Express.js application revolves around the Request and Response lifecycle. Understanding this flow is crucial for building robust APIs and web applications, managing asynchronous operations, and handling middleware correctly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: The Incoming Client Request"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The lifecycle begins when a client (such as a browser or mobile app) sends an HTTP request to your server. This request contains headers, parameters, query strings, and optionally a body. Express wraps this incoming Node.js http.IncomingMessage object into the 'req' (request) object."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Middleware Execution and the Next Function"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before reaching your final route handler, the 'req' and 'res' objects pass through any registered middleware functions. Middleware can modify the request, execute validation checks, or terminate the lifecycle early. The 'next()' function is responsible for passing control to the next middleware in the stack."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Application-level middleware
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log("Incoming Request: " + req.method + " " + req.url);
  next();
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Route Handling and Business Logic"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the request navigates through the middleware chain, it matches a specific route definition. Here, your application executes business logic, interacts with databases, and prepares data for the client."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res, next) => {
  const userId = req.params.id;
  // Simulate fetching user from database
  const user = { id: userId, name: "John Doe" };

  // Attach user to request object or pass to response
  res.locals.user = user;
  next();
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Sending the Response"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The lifecycle concludes when the server sends data back to the client using methods on the 'res' (response) object, such as res.send(), res.json(), or res.render(). Once a response method is called, the lifecycle is terminated, and no further modifications can be made to headers or body."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = res.locals.user;

  // Terminate the lifecycle by sending JSON response
  res.status(200).json({
    success: true,
    data: user
  });
});"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Rules of the Lifecycle"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always terminate the request-response cycle by sending a response to prevent hanging connections."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never call 'next()' after a response has already been sent, as this will throw a headers already sent error."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use error-handling middleware (functions with four arguments: err, req, res, next) to catch asynchronous and synchronous errors thrown during the lifecycle."}]
